A||N||3||Manta||Gas Cap Cable replacement||||5||
Z||000000||Manta||06-07-2000||03:08 AM||||The cable for my gas cap door release broke. <BR>Does anyone know the easiest way to replace it?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Monkeyman||06-07-2000||09:29 AM||||Didn't know they had one.  Neither my '85 nor my '86 have one.||||reg||1
Z||000002||prof bobo||06-08-2000||01:14 AM||||Funny you should ask.  I've got an '85 with 300K + miles on it, am now stripping it to the bone to repaint, replace, refurbish (already on my 2nd engine and 4th clutch).  I just took the back half of the body off tonight.  The inside release mechanism doesn't have to be removed.  The outside door components are held in with a few screws.  The bad news is that where the cable passes from the passenger compartment through the body, it is held by a retainer which can only be released if you remove the little quarter window -which involves removing the interior trim panel (one phillips screw, the seatbelt ancor bolt and prying loose a plastic clip) then the outer panel is held in by a small nut (look for a 1" hole in the inside frame for access), a small phillps screw at the bottom edge, and some clips that can freed via _gentle_ prying.  BTW, are you sure the CABLE is broken (which looks pretty rugged to me, indeed, I've used a LOT of gas in that car) and not the plastic parts it attaches to at either end?  If I were facing this job I'd probably attempt something expedient like trying to run a new 'inner cable' using a guitar string (.022-.030 range) and some on-the-spot engineered attaching scheme on the ends.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Manta||06-08-2000||02:06 PM||||The cable is still attached at the door. I think your right when you said it might be the plastic lever that broke instead of the cable. Thanks for the info on the clips that I have to pry up.||||reg||1
